WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.300
One sentence: setting a learning intention.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:09.170
Every January, Dimas writes the same line and sticks it on his wall: this year, I will get better at data analysis.

3
00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:15.550
By March, the note is still there. Nothing else has changed.

4
00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:22.450
The problem is not his effort. It is the sentence. “Better” is an adjective. It gives him nothing to check.

5
00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:31.100
So he crosses it out. The new sentence has a result he can count, and a date: by 30 June, three finished projects, shown to a mentor.

6
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:37.360
Next, time. His first plan is two hours every day. It survives nine days.

7
00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.620
His second plan is smaller: three sessions of forty minutes, booked in his calendar like classes he cannot skip.

8
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:49.220
A modest plan you keep beats an ambitious one you imagine.

9
00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:55.480
Last, a signal. Every Sunday evening, Dimas checks one number: sessions kept this week.

10
00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:03.050
Three of three, carry on. One of three, make the plan smaller, not the guilt bigger.

11
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:11.920
Put together, a working intention has four parts: a result you can count, a date, a weekly time budget you can defend, and one signal you check every Sunday.

12
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:19.320
Intentions with a number and a date get finished. Intentions with adjectives get abandoned.

13
00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.120
Now it is your turn. Write your one sentence for The Map: a result, a date, a weekly budget and a Sunday signal.
